// **A file-scope mutable variable** (ADR-0186) — the construct `modules/Simp` needs so that a
// renderer can hold its own state instead of threading a handle through every call.
//
// # Why this file exists at all
//
// Until this wave, `counter: s64 = 5;` at file scope was legal to *write* and impossible to *use*:
// any body that touched it was refused with E0245, *"a file-level item has no value until jr-vm"*.
// ADR-0178 gave that refusal an honest trapping stub. This is the wave that removes the reason for it.
//
// # What is pinned, and why each bit is separate
//
//   * a **scalar** global read before anything writes it, so its *initial value* is checked rather
//     than assumed — a global that reads zero when it should read five is the failure this feature
//     will really have, and it passes every type check;
//   * a **write, then a read in a different procedure**, which is the entire point of the construct
//     and the property a per-frame or per-body implementation would get wrong while looking correct;
//   * a global initialised by a **compile-time call** rather than a literal, since the initialiser is
//     a const-eval target and a call is the interesting case;
//   * an **aggregate** global, field-assigned and field-read, because a struct global takes the
//     projection path rather than the scalar one, and those are different code in all three engines;
//   * an **uninitialised** global (`---`), which must read as **zero** and not as undefined bytes:
//     ADR-0186 §2 makes that a promise rather than an accident of the loader, so it is asserted;
//   * a global **mutated in a loop**, which is what catches an engine that reloads a stale value or
//     an optimiser that forwards a store across the call that observes it.
//
// The three engines must agree, and this is a file where they easily might not: the VM bump-allocates
// a region, Cranelift emits a writable data object and takes its address with `symbol_value`, and LLVM
// emits an internal mutable global. Three different mechanisms for one meaning.
//
// Exit code 63 = 1 + 2 + 4 + 8 + 16 + 32, so one failing bit names itself.
#import "Basic";

// A scalar with a literal initialiser — the simplest form.
counter: s64 = 5;

// An initialiser that is a **call**, evaluated at compile time. There is no moment before `main` at
// which arbitrary code could run, so the value has to exist by then; this proves it does.
computed: s64 = triple(7);

// An aggregate global. Its fields are written and read through the projection path.
origin: Point;

// Explicitly uninitialised. ADR-0186 §2 promises **zero**, not undefined bytes, so that the native
// path and the VM cannot differ about a program that never assigns it first.
untouched: s64 = ---;

Point :: struct {
    x: s64;
    y: s64;
}

triple :: (n: s64) -> s64 {
    return n * 3;
}

// A *different* procedure from the one that reads it below. That separation is the test: a global
// exists so that two procedures share storage.
bump :: () {
    counter = counter + 1;
}

read_counter :: () -> s64 {
    return counter;
}

// Mutation in a loop, through a procedure call each time. An engine that caches the global in a
// register across the call, or an optimiser that forwards the store past it, gives the wrong total.
accumulate :: (times: s64) {
    i := 0;
    while i < times {
        bump();
        i = i + 1;
    }
}

main :: () {
    n := 0;

    // The initial value, read before any write.
    if read_counter() == 5 {
        n = n + 1;
    }

    // A write in one procedure, observed by another.
    bump();
    if read_counter() == 6 {
        n = n + 2;
    }

    // The compile-time call initialiser.
    if computed == 21 {
        n = n + 4;
    }

    // An aggregate global, field-assigned then field-read.
    origin.x = 3;
    origin.y = 4;
    if origin.x * origin.y == 12 {
        n = n + 8;
    }

    // `---` reads as zero.
    if untouched == 0 {
        n = n + 16;
    }

    // Ten more increments, each through a call.
    accumulate(10);
    if read_counter() == 16 {
        n = n + 32;
    }

    exit(n);
}
